                <pb facs="0435" n="337" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg337"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">723.2515/597: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Chile</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Shea</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-03-29T18:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 29,
                                1920—6 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>23. Your 490 [<hi rendition="#italic">49</hi>], March 27, 6 p.m.</p>
                <p>You may make a brief and very courteous reply to statement of Huneeus and may
                    authorize its publication.</p>
                <p>If contents of note of Huneeus permits you to do so, you may state in your reply
                    that you are “in cordial agreement with” the ideas expressed. You will also
                    state “the purpose of the Government of the United States has been to assure
                    itself of the cooperation of Chile in the maintenance of peace in South America,
                    and my Government has felt deep appreciation of the assurance of cooperation
                    given it by the Chilean Government.”</p>
                <p>Cable Department text and reply of note immediately.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Colby</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
